/*
	RMSTEST 6 — does LOOSE grouping check avoid_forest_zone per member, when
	TIGHT grouping only checks it once on an anchor?

	Test 1 established the tight-group mechanism: one member is placed, it has
	to pass the avoid restrictions, then the rest fill in from there unchecked.
	The open question is whether loose grouping differs — and whether the
	tight/loose split in guide:2122 / guide:2143 is the real rule after all,
	rather than the per-attribute table in docs/preview-design.md Sec.6.6.

	This map puts BOTH conditions in the SAME clearing on the SAME seed, so
	they are compared directly instead of across two runs:

	  STONE  12 objects, set_loose_grouping, group_placement_radius 6
	  GOLD   25 objects, set_tight_grouping          <- known behaviour, in-map
	                                                    reference

	Both carry avoid_forest_zone 6 in a 15x15 clearing (radius 7), where only a
	roughly 1-tile-radius patch at the clearing's centre satisfies that
	distance. So per-member checking is severely constrained and anchor-only
	checking is not.

	STONE is deliberately declared FIRST because the engine places commands in
	script order, first come first served — if GOLD ran first its 25 tight
	members would occupy the centre and starve the test.

	READ IT LIKE THIS  (look at the STONE piles only; GOLD is the reference)
	  Stone scattered widely, some piles near or against the tree line
	      -> loose ALSO checks only an anchor. The tight/loose split is not
	         real, and Sec.6.6's table collapses to one rule for both.
	  Stone confined to a tight knot at the clearing's centre, well clear of
	  the forest, and FEWER than 12 piles present
	      -> loose checks EVERY member. guide:2143 is right, the tight/loose
	         split IS the rule, and Sec.6.6's per-attribute table is wrong in
	         a third way: it is neither per-attribute nor uniformly anchor-only.
	  Stone absent entirely
	      -> per-member checking plus an unlucky anchor. Re-roll a few seeds
	         before concluding; absence across many seeds still means
	         per-member.

	Worth noting either way: if stone comes out SHORT of 12, that is
	guide:2143's documented "some group members fail to spawn", and it is what
	Sec.6.6's groupPartial failure bucket is meant to represent.

	SECOND QUESTION, same map, no extra work: you thought some attributes may
	check every member even under tight grouping. If the GOLD block here
	behaves exactly as it did in Test 1, avoid_forest_zone is not one of them.
	Identifying which attributes DO would need a separate map per attribute,
	so if you have a candidate in mind, name it and I will write that test
	directly rather than guessing.

	RUN ON: Tiny, 4 players. Four clearings = four samples per seed.
*/

<PLAYER_SETUP>
random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
base_terrain FOREST

create_player_lands
{
	terrain_type GRASS
	base_size 7
	number_of_tiles 1
	border_fuzziness 0
	circle_radius 33
}

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

/* THE TEST: loose, spread over radius 6, same avoidance as Test 1. */
create_object STONE
{
	number_of_objects 12
	set_loose_grouping
	group_placement_radius 6
	set_gaia_object_only
	set_place_for_every_player
	avoid_forest_zone 6
}

/* IN-MAP REFERENCE: the tight group from Test 1, unchanged. */
create_object GOLD
{
	number_of_objects 25
	set_tight_grouping
	set_gaia_object_only
	set_place_for_every_player
	avoid_forest_zone 6
}
